2186051668 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488564 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054399 1071 "What is the solution to $7z \\lt -21$?" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186054399 1071 "What is the solution to $7z \\lt -21?$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054399 1074 "The variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $7$. To isolate $z$, we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $7$. Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7z & < - 21 \\\\ \\frac{7z}{7} & < \\frac{-21}{7} \\\\ \\frac{7z}{7} & < - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7z}{7} & < - 3 \\\\ \\frac{7z}{7} & < - 3 \\\\ z & < - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z \\lt -3$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186054399 1074 "The variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $7.$ To isolate $z$, we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $7.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7z & <-21 \\\\ \\frac{7z}{7} & <\\frac{-21}{7} \\\\ \\frac{7z}{7} & <-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7z}{7} & <-3 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}z}{\\cancel{7}} & <-3 \\\\ z & <-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z \\lt -3.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054400 1071 "Solve the inequality $-6x \\leq -7 .$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054400 1071 "Solve the inequality $-6x \\leq -7$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186054400 1074 "The variable $x$ is being *multiplied* by $-6$. To isolate $x$, we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-6$. Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-6x & \\le - 7 \\\\ \\frac{-6x}{-6} & \\ge\\frac{-7}{-6} \\\\ \\frac{-6x}{-6} & \\ge\\frac{7}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-6x}{-6} & \\ge \\frac{7}{6} \\\\ \\frac{-6x}{-6} & \\ge \\frac{7}{6} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{7}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\geq \\dfrac{7}{6}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186054400 1074 "The variable $x$ is being *multiplied* by $-6.$ To isolate $x,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-6.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-7 \\\\ \\frac{-6x}{-6}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-7}{-6} \\\\ \\frac{-6x}{-6}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6x}{-6} & \\ge \\frac{7}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-6}x}{\\cancel{-6}} & \\ge \\frac{7}{6} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\geq \\dfrac{7}{6}.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054401 1071 "Solve the inequality $-\\dfrac{y}{8} \\leq -4$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186054401 1071 "Solve the inequality $-\\dfrac{y}{8} \\leq -4.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054401 1074 "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $8$. To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-8$.\n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{y}{8} & \\le - 4 \\\\ (-8) \\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8}) & \\ge(-8) \\cdot (-4) \\\\ (-8) \\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8}) & \\ge32\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(-8) \\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8}) & \\ge 32 \\\\ (-8) \\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8}) & \\ge 32 \\\\ 8 \\cdot \\frac{y}{8} & \\ge 32\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $8$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\cdot \\frac{y}{8} & \\ge 32 \\\\ \\frac{8y}{8} & \\ge 32 \\\\ \\frac{8y}{8} & \\ge 32 \\\\ y & \\ge 32\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny \\geq 32\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186054401 1074 "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $8.$ To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-8.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{8}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-4 \\\\ (-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-8)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ (-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8}) & \\ge 32 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}8)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{y}{8}) & \\ge 32 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{y}{8} & \\ge 32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $8$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot \\frac{y}{8} & \\ge 32 \\\\ \\frac{8y}{8} & \\ge 32 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{8}y}{\\cancel{8}} & \\ge 32 \\\\ y & \\ge 32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y \\geq 32.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054402 1071 "Solve the inequality $-9 \\leq -3z$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186054402 1071 "Solve the inequality $-9 \\leq -3z.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054402 1074 "The variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $-3$. To isolate $z$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-3$. Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & \\le - 3z \\\\ \\frac{-9}{-3} & \\ge\\frac{-3z}{-3} \\\\ 3 & \\ge\\frac{-3z}{-3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\ge \\frac{-3z}{-3} \\\\ 3 & \\ge \\frac{-3z}{-3} \\\\ 3 & \\ge z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember that we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\nz \\leq 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nz \\leq 3\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186054402 1074 "The variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $-3.$ To isolate $z,$ we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-3.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-3z \\\\ \\frac{-9}{-3}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-3z}{-3} \\\\ 3\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-3z}{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel the common factor $-3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & \\ge \\frac{-3z}{-3} \\\\ 3 & \\ge \\frac{\\cancel{-3}z}{\\cancel{-3}} \\\\ 3 & \\ge z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nz \\leq 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z \\leq 3.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489199 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489277 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489213 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489218 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489177 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489254 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489248 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489269 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489281 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489283 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489193 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489197 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054772 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489185 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489220 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489266 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054787 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489221 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054787 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489294 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489188 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054862 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489287 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186054900 1050 420.0 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186055007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186055007 1071 "$2u+(5u+v)\\cdot (-4)=$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186055007 1071 "What is $2u + (5u + v) \\cdot (-4)$?" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186055007 1074 "First, we distribute the $(-4)$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}2u+(5u+v)\\cdot (-4) & = \\\\ 2u+5u\\cdot (-4)+v\\cdot (-4) & = \\\\ 2u+(-20u)+(-4v) & = \\\\ 2u-20u-4v & .\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u-20u-4v & = \\\\ (2u-20u)-4v & = \\\\ -18u-4v & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186055023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186055023 1071 "Solve the inequality $9 \\ge - \\frac{x}{4}$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186055023 1071 "Solve the inequality $9\\ge -\\frac{x}{4}.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186055023 1074 "The variable $x$ is being *divided* by $4.$ To isolate $x$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-4$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ (-4)\\cdot 9\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ -36\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36\\,\\, & \\le (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ -36 & \\le (\\cancel{-}4)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ -36 & \\le 4\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel the common factor $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-36 & \\le 4\\cdot \\frac{x}{4} \\\\ -36 & \\le \\frac{4x}{4} \\\\ -36 & \\le \\frac{\\cancel{4}x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ -36 & \\le x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -36\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -36.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186056020 1064 "Let's consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx + 4 < 7\n$$\n\nTo **solve** this inequality, we need to isolate the variable $x$ on one side.\n\nInequalities are just like equations because they can be solved using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nSo, to solve this inequality, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & <7 \\\\ x+4-4 & <7-4 \\\\ x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $x < 3.$ This tells us that any value of $x$ smaller than $3$ satisfies the original inequality.\n\nWe can represent the solution to this inequality using a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/55/556401d043fbd285a42b257e3c32f0a858377355674c264b90396e43ca8a8a13.png)\n\nLet's take a look at an inequality that we can solve using the multiplication principle." 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186056020 1064 "Let's consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx + 4 < 7\n$$\n\nTo **solve** this inequality, we need to isolate the variable $x$ on one side.\n\nInequalities are just like equations because they can be solved using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nSo, to solve this inequality, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 4 & < 7 \\\\ x + 4 - 4 & < 7 - 4 \\\\ x & < 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $x < 3$. This tells us that any value of $x$ smaller than $3$ satisfies the original inequality.\n\nWe can represent the solution to this inequality using a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/55/556401d043fbd285a42b257e3c32f0a858377355674c264b90396e43ca8a8a13.png)\n\nLet's take a look at an inequality that we can solve using the multiplication principle." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186056021 1064 "We've seen that solving linear inequalities is similar to solving linear equations.\n\nHowever, there is one additional rule that we need to remember when solving inequalities:\n\n*When multiplying or dividing both sides by a **negative number**, we flip the inequality sign.*\n\nWhen we \"flip the inequality sign,\" we switch the direction of the inequality symbol. For example:\n\n- \"$\\color{blue}<$\" becomes \"$\\color{red}>$\" and vice versa\n\n- \"$\\color{blue}\\leq$\" becomes \"$\\color{red}\\geq$\" and vice versa\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n- 3x \\gt 9\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality using the multiplication principle. However, when we divide both sides by $-3,$ we have to flip the inequality sign. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x\\,\\, & >\\,\\,9 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{9}{-3} \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-3 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-3}x}{\\cancel{-3}}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-3 \\\\ x\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\lt -3.$\n\nWe'll discuss why we need to flip the inequality sign at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying this idea." 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186056021 1064 "We've seen that solving linear inequalities is similar to solving linear equations.\n\nHowever, there is one additional rule that we need to remember when solving inequalities:\n\n> *When multiplying or dividing both sides by a **negative number**, we flip the inequality sign.*\n\nWhen we \"flip the inequality sign,\" we switch the direction of the inequality symbol. For example:\n\n- \"$\\color{blue}<$\" becomes \"$\\color{red}>$\" and vice versa\n- \"$\\color{blue}\\leq$\" becomes \"$\\color{red}\\geq$\" and vice versa\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-3x \\gt 9\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality using the multiplication principle. However, when we divide both sides by $-3$, we have to flip the inequality sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3x & >9 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & <\\frac{9}{-3} \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & < - 3 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & < - 3 \\\\ x & < - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\lt -3$.\n\nWe'll discuss why we need to flip the inequality sign at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying this idea." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186056022 1064 "We'll now learn why we must flip the inequality sign when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number.\n\nTo help us, let's consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n6 > 0\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement, and it remains true if we multiply or divide both sides by a positive number.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $2$ gives $12 > 0$, which is *true*.$\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Dividing the inequality by $2$ gives $3 > 0$, which is also *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nHowever, if we multiply or divide both sides by a negative number, *without* flipping the inequality sign, we get a false statement.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $-2$ *without flipping the inequality sign* gives $-12 > 0$, which is *false*. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- Dividing the inequality by $-2$ *without flipping the inequality sign* gives $-3 > 0$, which is also *false*. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo, whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we need to flip the sign of the inequality for it to remain true.\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $-2$ *and flipping the inequality sign* gives $-12 < 0$, which is *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Dividing the inequality by $-2$ *and flipping the sign* gives $-3 < 0$, which is also *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186056022 1064 "We'll now learn why we must flip the inequality sign when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number. \n\nTo help us, let's consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n6 > 0\n$$\n\n \n\nThis is a true statement, and it remains true if we multiply or divide both sides by a positive number. \n\nFor example:\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $2$ gives $12 > 0,$ which is *true*.$\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- Dividing the inequality by $2$ gives $3 > 0,$ which is also *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nHowever, if we multiply or divide both sides by a negative number, *without* flipping the inequality sign, we get a false statement.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $-2$ *without flipping the inequality sign* gives $-12 > 0,$ which is *false*. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\n- Dividing the inequality by $-2$ *without flipping the inequality sign* gives $-3 > 0,$ which is also *false*. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo, whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we need to flip the sign of the inequality for it to remain true.\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $-2$ *and flipping the inequality sign* gives $-12 < 0,$ which is *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- Dividing the inequality by $-2$ *and flipping the sign* gives $-3 < 0,$ which is also *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062750 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062751 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062752 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062753 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062754 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488748 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488760 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488823 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488865 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186062749 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488892 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186062749 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488892 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186063247 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186063248 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186063249 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186063250 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186063251 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488608 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488616 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488659 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488844 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063246 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488936 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063246 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488616 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063624 1078 #edb/ref 17592186063627 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063969 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $2x - y = 5y - z$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063969 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $2x - y = 5y - z.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063969 1074 "We need to isolate $x$, so we will treat the other variables $y$ and $z$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $x$.\n\nFirst, we add $y$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x - y &= 5y - z \\\\[5pt] 2x - y + y &= 5y - z + y \\\\[5pt] 2x &= 6y - z \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x &= 6y - z \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {2x} 2 &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2} \\\\[5pt] x &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063969 1074 "We need to isolate $x,$ so we will treat the other variables $y$ and $z$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $x.$\n\nFirst, we add $y$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x - y &= 5y - z \\[5pt]\n\t\t2x - y + y &= 5y - z + y \\[5pt]\n\t\t2x &= 6y - z\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we divide both sides by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2x &= 6y - z \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2x} 2 &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2} \\[5pt]\n\t\tx &= \\dfrac{6y - z} {2}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063970 1074 "We need to isolate $m$, so we will treat the other variables $E$ and $C$ as constants. Since $m$ is being multiplied by $C$, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE &= mC \\\\\n\\frac{E}{C} &= \\frac{mC}{C} \\\\\n\\frac{E}{C} &= m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac E C\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that\n\n$$\nC\\neq 0\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063970 1074 "We need to isolate $m,$ so we will treat the other variables $E$ and $C$ as constants. Since $m$ is being multiplied by $C,$ we need to divide both sides of the equation by $C$:\n![](images/27/27683350a3eb25bd3962e12724a01f09350d4c62ba85afb137df8cff27992be7.svg)\nThus, $m = \\dfrac E C.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $C\\neq 0.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063971 1071 "The total energy $E$ of a body moving with a (squared) velocity $V$ can be approximated using the equation $E = mC + \\dfrac{1}{2}mV$. Find an expression for $V$ in terms of the other quantities." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063971 1071 "The total energy $E$ of a body moving with a (squared) velocity $V$ can be approximated using the equation $E = mC + \\dfrac{1}{2}mV.$ Find an expression for $V$ in terms of the other quantities." 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186063971 1074 "We need to isolate $V$, so we will treat the other variables $E$, $C$, and $m$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $V$.\n\nFirst, we subtract $mC$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE &= mC + \\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\\nE - mC &= mC + \\frac{1}{2}mV - mC \\\\\nE - mC &= \\frac{1}{2}mV\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $2$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE - mC &= \\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\\n2 \\cdot (E - mC) &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\\n2(E - mC) &= mV\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide by $m$ to get $V$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(E - mC) &= mV \\\\\n(2(E - mC))/(m) &= \\frac{mV}{m} \\\\\n(2(E - mC))/(m) &= V\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{2(E-mC)}{m}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that\n\n$$\nm\\neq 0\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186063971 1074 "We need to isolate $V,$ so we will treat the other variables $E,$ $C,$ and $m$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $V.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $mC$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =mC+\\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\ E-mC & =mC+\\frac{1}{2}mV-mC \\\\ E-mC & =\\frac{1}{2}mV\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $2$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-mC & =\\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\ 2\\cdot (E-mC) & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}mV \\\\ 2(E-mC) & =mV\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide by $m$ to get $V$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(E-mC) & =mV \\\\ \\frac{2(E-mC)}{m} & =\\frac{mV}{m} \\\\ \\frac{2(E-mC)}{m} & =V\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $V = \\dfrac{2(E-mC)}{m}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $m\\neq 0.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489183 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489233 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489239 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489182 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489192 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489189 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064195 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489276 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064224 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489209 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064224 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489237 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489203 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489229 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489259 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489293 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489187 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489231 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489241 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489288 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489240 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489256 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489260 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489275 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489184 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489234 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489289 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064429 1050 1080.0 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064443 1050 420.0 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064782 1071 "Make $c$ the subject of the equation $-6a = 9b - 2c$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186064782 1071 "Make $c$ the subject of the equation $-6a=9b-2c.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064782 1074 "We need to isolate $c,$ so we will treat the other variables $a$ and $b$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $c.$\n\nFirst, we add $2c$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6a & =9b-2c \\\\ -6a+2c & =9b-2c+2c \\\\ -6a+2c & =9b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $6a$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6a+2c & =9b \\\\ -6a+2c+6a & =9b+6a \\\\ 2c & =9b+6a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =9b+6a \\\\ \\frac{2c}{2} & =\\frac{9b+6a}{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{9b+6a}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064865 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{4x^{2} - 17x + 4}{x - 4}dx$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186064865 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-17x+4}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064865 1074 "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n|  | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $-17$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $16$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-17x+4}{x-4}=4x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-17x+4}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4x-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064928 1071 "${(-\\frac{100}{124})}^{0}=$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186064928 1071 "What is $(-\\frac{100}{124})^{0}$?" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186064928 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{100}{124})}^{0}=1.\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186065096 1061 1707 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186065096 1061 630729 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186065102 1061 1708 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186065102 1061 630730 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186065103 1061 1711 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186065103 1061 630733 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186065113 1061 1714 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186065113 1061 630736 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186065434 1064 "Sometimes we might be given an equation that contains more than one variable and asked to isolate one of the variables. This means that we need to put the variable we're interested in on one side of the equation (on its own) and everything else on the other.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to isolate $n$ in the equation\n\n$$\n2n - k = q + 5k\n$$\n\nThe equation has three variables, $n$, $k$, and $q$. However, we will treat the variables $k$ and $q$ as constants because we are solving for $n$ only. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $n\\mathbin{.}$\n\nFirst, we add $k$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2n - k &= q + 5k \\\\[5pt] 2n - k + k &= q + 5k +k \\\\[5pt] 2n &= q + 6k \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2n &= q + 6k \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {2n} {2} &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2} \\\\ n &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the equation is now solved for $n$, we say that we've made $n$ **the subject** of the equation." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186065434 1064 "Sometimes we might be given an equation that contains more than one variable and asked to isolate one of the variables. This means that we need to put the variable we're interested in on one side of the equation (on its own) and everything else on the other.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to isolate $n$ in the equation\n\n$$\n2n - k = q + 5k.\n$$\n\nThe equation has three variables, $n,$ $k,$ and $q.$ However, we will treat the variables $k$ and $q$ as constants because we are solving for $n$ only. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $n\\mathbin{.}$ \n\nFirst, we add $k$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2n - k &= q + 5k \\[5pt]\n\t\t2n - k + k &= q + 5k +k \\[5pt]\n\t\t2n &= q + 6k \\\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2n &= q + 6k \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2n} {2} &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2} \\\nn &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2}\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nBecause the equation is now solved for $n$, we say that we've made $n$ **the subject** of the equation." 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186065438 1064 "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}\\,\\textrm d x\\,?$\n\nWe need to write the integrand so that it's something we can integrate. One way to do this is to divide $3x+2$ by $x+2$ using polynomial or synthetic division. We'll use synthetic division because it's faster. \n\nTo rewrite $\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2},$ we complete a synthetic division table.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ |\n\nThis tells us that $\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}$ can be decomposed into a quotient and remainder as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}= 3 - \\dfrac{4}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can integrate the function, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3-\\frac{4}{x+2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-4\\operatorname{ln}|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186065438 1064 "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}\\,\\textrm d x\\,?$\n\nWe need to write the integrand so that it's something we can integrate. One way to do this is to divide $3x+2$ by $x+2$ using polynomial or synthetic division. We'll use synthetic division because it's faster. \n\nTo rewrite $\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2},$ we complete a synthetic division table.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ |\n\nThis tells us that $\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}$ can be decomposed into a quotient and remainder as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}= 3 - \\dfrac{4}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can integrate the function, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3-\\frac{4}{x+2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186068679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489178 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186068680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489258 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186068680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489267 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186073676 1038 #edb/ref 1759218648918