78 #edb/ref 17592186488794 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186133788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489238 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186133788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489280 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186134243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186134243 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r = sv + tw$, where $s, t ∈ R$, and\n$v = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186134243 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186134243 1074 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 19\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}19 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186138368 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489228 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186144303 1078 #edb/ref 17592186144304 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147198 1074 "The number of infected files, $y(t)$, grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4$.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 10$ is the initial number of infected files\n- $b = 4$ is the growth factor (the number of infected files quadruples)\n- $t=$ the time (in hours)\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny = ab^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)= 10 \\cdot 4^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the number of files infected after $6$ hours, we substitute $t=6$ into the above:\n\n$$\ny(6) = 10 \\cdot 4^{6} = 40\\,960\n$$\n\nTherefore, the computer will have $40\\,960$ infected files after $6$ hours." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186147198 1074 "The number of infected files, $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 10$ is the initial number of infected files\n\n- $b = 4$ is the growth factor (the number of infected files quadruples)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours)\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny = ab^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)= 10 \\cdot 4^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the number of files infected after $6$ hours, we  substitute $t=6$ into the above:\n\n$$\ny(6) = 10 \\cdot 4^{6} = 40\\,960\n$$\n\nTherefore, the computer will have $40\\,960$ infected files after $6$ hours." 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147199 1071 "In the week of opening, the grocery store sold $20$ kilograms of apples. After that, the weekly sales increased by $25 \\%$ every week.\n\nFind a function that models the total weight of apples sold in week $t$ after opening, and use this function to find the total amount of apples sold in the $4$th week." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186147199 1071 "In the week of opening, the grocery store sold $20$ kilograms of apples. After that, the weekly sales increased by $25 \\%$ every week. \n\nFind a function that models the total weight of apples sold in week $t$ after opening, and use this function to find the total amount of apples sold in the $4$th week." 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147199 1074 "The number of kilograms of apples sold $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $25 \\%$.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 20$ is the total weight of the apples (in kilograms) sold initially\n- $r = 0.25$ is the growth rate ($25 \\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after opening\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny = 20 \\cdot (1 + 0.25)^{t}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the total weight of apples sold in week $4$, we evaluate the above for $t=4$:\n\n$$\ny(4) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{4} \\approx 49\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $49$ kilograms of apples were sold in week $4$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186147199 1074 "The number of kilograms of apples sold $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $25 \\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 20$ is the total weight of the apples (in kilograms) sold initially\n\n- $r = 0.25$ is the growth rate ($25 \\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after opening\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny   = 20 \\cdot (1 + 0.25)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total weight of apples sold in week $4,$ we evaluate the above for $t=4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny(4) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{4} \\approx 49\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $49$ kilograms of apples were sold in week $4.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147215 1074 "The number of listeners $L(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190\\%$.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 80$ is the total number of listeners initially\n- $r = 1.9$ is the growth rate ($190\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after release\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nL = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $L(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL = 80 \\cdot (1 + 1.9)^{t}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nL(t) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of listeners after $2$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=2{:}$\n\n$$\nL(2) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{2} \\approx 673\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of listeners after $2$ weeks is $673$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186147215 1074 "The number of listeners $L(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 80$ is the total number of listeners initially\n\n- $r = 1.9$ is the growth rate ($190\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after release\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nL = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $L(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL = 80 \\cdot (1 + 1.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nL(t) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of listeners after $2$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=2{:}$\n\n$$\nL(2) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{2} \\approx 673\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of listeners after $2$ weeks is $673.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147415 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489236 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147473 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489214 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147473 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489253 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147474 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489206 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147474 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489252 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147475 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489179 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147563 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489204 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147563 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489246 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147572 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489175 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147671 1050 420.0 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186147767 1074 "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $250%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=90$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=2.5$ is the growth rate ($250%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after upload\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=90\\cdot (1+2.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=90\\cdot (3.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of views after $3$ hours, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=90\\cdot (3.5)^{3}≈3859\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of views after $3$ hours is $3859.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186148683 1064 "When dealing with exponential growth, it's more common to be given a **growth rate** instead of a growth factor. A growth rate is usually expressed as a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that an investor invests $\\[math]500$ dollars in the shares of some high-growth companies. It's expected that the investment will grow by $35\\%$ each year. Can we write a function that describes the value of the investment after $t$ years?\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=\\[/math]500$ is the **initial value** of the investment\n- $r = 0.35$ is the **growth rate** (this is simply $35\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n- $t$ is the time (in years)\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the value of the investment after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our values for $a$ and $r$, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1+0.35)^t\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we arrive at\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1.35)^t\n$$\n\nWhen creating exponential growth functions, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If you're given a **growth rate** $r$ (usually expressed as a percent), then use the formula $y = a(1+r)^t$.\n- If you're given a **growth factor** $b$, then use the formula $y = ab^t$." 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186148683 1064 "When dealing with exponential growth, it's more common to be given a **growth rate** instead of a growth factor. A growth rate is usually expressed as a percent.\n\nFor example, suppose that an investor invests $\\[math]500$ dollars in the shares of some high-growth companies. It's expected that the investment will grow by $35\\%$ each year. Can we write a function that describes the value of the investment after $t$ years? \n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=\\[/math]500$ is the **initial value** of the investment\n\n- $r = 0.35$ is the **growth rate** (this is simply $35\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t$ is the time (in years)\n\nLet $y(t)$ be the value of the investment after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our values for $a$ and $r,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1+0.35)^t.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we arrive at\n\n$$\ny(t) = 500(1.35)^t.\n$$\n\nWhen creating exponential growth functions, the key takeaway is this:\n\n- If you're given a **growth rate** $r$ (usually expressed as a percent), then use the formula $y = a(1+r)^t.$\n\n- If you're given a **growth factor** $b,$ then use the formula $y = ab^t.$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186148684 1064 "Suppose that there are $120$ deer in a wildlife reserve. If the deer population doubles every year, can we find a function that gives the number of deer after $t$ years?\n\nFirst, let's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 120$ is the **initial number** of deer\n- $b =2$ is the **growth factor** (equal to $2$ because the number of deer doubles every year)\n- $t=$ the time (in years)\n\nWe let $y(t)$ be the number of deer in the reserve after $t$ years. The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 120\\cdot 2^t\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to find the number of deer in the reserve for any value of\n\n$$\nt \\geq 0\n$$\n\nFor example, to find the number of deer after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(3) &= 120 \\cdot 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 960\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there will be $960$ deer after $3$ years.\n\nFinally, notice that substituting $t=0$ into the formula gives the initial number of deer:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= 120 \\cdot 2^{0} \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186148684 1064 "Suppose that there are $120$ deer in a wildlife reserve. If the deer population doubles every year, can we find a function that gives the number of deer after $t$ years? \n\nFirst, let's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 120$ is the **initial number** of deer\n\n- $b =2$ is the **growth factor** (equal to $2$ because the number of deer doubles every year)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in years)\n\nWe let $y(t)$ be the number of deer in the reserve after $t$ years.  The formula for $y(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $a$ and $b,$ we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 120\\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to find the number of deer in the reserve for any value of $t \\geq 0.$ For example, to find the number of deer after $3$ years, we substitute $t=3$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(3) & =120\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there will be $960$ deer after $3$ years.\n\nFinally, notice that substituting $t=0$ into the formula gives the initial number of deer:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =120\\cdot {2}^{0} \\\\  & =120\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =120\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186150563 1078 #edb/ref 17592186150566 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186152676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186152676 1071 "Which of the following numbers are rational?\n\n1. $3.624$\n2. $0.25^(―)$\n3. $7.526380…$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 false] [17592186152676 1071 "Which of the following numbers are rational?\n\nI. $3.624$\n\nII. $0.\\overset{―}{25}$\n\nIII. $7.526380…$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186152676 1074 "Let's examine each of our numbers in turn.\n\n- $3.624$ is a rational number because it can be expressed as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n3.624=\\frac{3\\,624}{1\\,000}\n$$\n\n- $0.\\overset{―}{25}$ is a rational number. The notation $\\overset{―}{25}$ tells us that the digits $2$ and $5$ after the decimal point repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overset{―}{25}=0.2525252525…\n$$\n\nAny decimal with repeating digits can be represented as a ratio of two integers. In this case,\n\n$$\n0.\\overset{―}{25}=\\frac{25}{99}.\n$$\n\n- $7.526380…$ is not a rational number. The notation \"$…$\" tells us that digits after the decimal point extend forever. However, there are no repeating 